#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A merge that DELETES live code reports nothing. This counts what
survived it.

    tools/merge-census-gate census <treeish>              # the census itself
    tools/merge-census-gate compare <before> <after>      # what vanished
    tools/merge-census-gate check [<merge-commit>]        # what a hook runs
    tools/merge-census-gate pending                       # the uncommitted merge

WHY IT EXISTS. Every keep-both hazard this repository has paid for was
about a CONFLICT — two sides editing one region, a human choosing badly.
This is the other half, and it is silent by construction: when git
resolves cleanly by taking one side's REMOVAL of code the other side
still has, there is no conflict, no marker, and no message. A revert of
live code is textually indistinguishable from a deliberate deletion, so
git is not doing anything wrong; it simply cannot tell, and neither can
a reviewer reading a merge that reports "Auto-merging" and stops.

On 2026-08-07 `fe4d8179` on `claude/019-asset-packs` reverted the
CDEF-attribution work its own immediate predecessor had landed, across
seven files, in a commit whose subject is about `hello.machine`. Merging
that lane auto-resolved nearly all of the revert with no conflict. It
would have gone red at the gate only because a sibling lane's
`scene_json_test.c` assertions happened to survive with it — luck, not a
guard. Had the test file been reverted too the merge would have been
green and the product quietly worse.

WHAT A CENSUS IS. Every top-level definition this tree can name -- C
functions, prototypes, macros, tags; Swift declarations; Python defs and
classes; the heading structure of every published document -- derived
from the tree itself at the moment it is asked, never from a list. The
same standard `CommandParityTests` holds: a gate that maintains an
inventory is a second place to be wrong.

THREE VERDICTS, against the merge base, and only two of them refuse.

  DROPPED (refuses).  A definition a parent has, that NEITHER side
      removed relative to the base, and the merge does not. Nobody
      deleted it and it is gone, so the resolution lost it. That is the
      two keep-both truncations already in this repository's ledger: six
      duplicate Python `def`s that still parsed, and a docs entry gutted
      by keeping a newer heading and dropping an older body. In both, the
      shared trailing context sat OUTSIDE the conflict hunk, so keeping
      both sides of the hunk still lost it.

  IMPORTED SELF-REVERT (refuses).  A commit in `HEAD..MERGE_HEAD` that
      `tools/self-revert-gate` flags: it undoes its own predecessor and
      says nothing about it. This is the half that catches `fe4d8179`,
      and it is a separate question from the census on purpose -- the
      census saw exactly ONE name move in that seven-file revert
      (`Scene.swift`'s `cdef`), and a gate that depended on that would be
      depending on luck twice over. Only commits the merge IMPORTS are
      asked about; history already on the first parent is not re-refused,
      or an arc goes red forever for a commit nobody can now change. And
      a merge whose own message NAMES the commit passes: round 7's real
      merge did exactly that, resolved all seven files by hand, and must
      not be refused for carrying the sha in its ancestry.

  REMOVED BY A SIDE (reports, never refuses).  A definition one parent
      removed after the base, honoured by the merge. A deliberate
      deletion and a revert are the same bytes here -- that is the whole
      difficulty -- so refusing would refuse every honest deletion in the
      fleet. Each name is instead ATTRIBUTED to the commit that removed
      it and grouped under its subject, because eighty bare names is a
      list nobody reads and eighty names under six subjects is six
      judgements. `--strict` refuses on it, for a quiet fleet.

WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, and the list is longer than the covered one:

  * Anything INSIDE a function body. `fe4d8179` reverted three `switch`
    arms in `now_cdef_role` -- the actual defect, an attribution that
    names every check box in OS 9's control panels a push button -- and
    the function is still called `now_cdef_role` before and after. A
    census counts names, and the name did not move.
  * Signatures. `put_coverage_claim` lost a parameter in the same revert
    and is one symbol either way.
  * Content. A document heading that survives with its body gutted is a
    heading either way, unless the body held headings of its own.
  * Renames. A file the merge renamed loses every symbol it had; those
    are reported at FILE level and not symbol level, so a rename reads as
    one line rather than forty, and a rename that also drops a function
    reads as one line rather than two.

So the census half is a gate against a merge SILENTLY DROPPING A NAME,
and it is not a gate against a merge silently changing what the name
does. The second one is what tests are for, and the reason the first is
worth having anyway is that the tests only fire if something else
survived to disagree. The imported-self-revert half is what covers the
case where nothing did.

Mutation evidence: `tools/mirror-gate-tests/test_merge_census_gate.py`,
in `scripts/test-native`. Five mutations were watched to fail it,
including one that stops censusing Swift properties -- which is what
makes the replay of the real 2026-08-07 auto-merge load-bearing rather
than decorative.
"""

import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

# Trees that are not this project's live code. `mirror/.claude/worktrees/`
# is five whole nested agent checkouts, 3,789 tracked files carrying their
# own copies of MirrorKit (docs/open-issues.md, 2026-08-07): censusing them
# would triple every count and make a real loss unfindable among six copies
# of the same name. `archive/` is by definition code that was removed on
# purpose. `qemu/` is a large upstream clone.
SKIP_PREFIXES = (
    "mirror/.claude/",
    "archive/",
    "qemu/",
    ".claude/worktrees/",
)

C_EXT = (".c", ".h")
SWIFT_EXT = (".swift",)
PY_EXT = (".py",)
MD_EXT = (".md",)

# Words that begin a statement, so a line matching the function-definition
# shape with one of these in the name slot is a call or a control structure,
# not a definition.
C_NOT_A_NAME = {
    "if", "for", "while", "switch", "return", "sizeof", "else", "do",
    "case", "goto", "defined", "typedef", "struct", "union", "enum",
    "static", "const", "extern", "inline", "void", "int", "char",
    "unsigned", "signed", "short", "long", "float", "double", "register",
    "volatile", "auto",
}

# EVERY PATTERN HERE IS LINEAR ON PURPOSE. The first version of C_FUNC was
# `^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_ \t\*]*?(\w+)\s*\(` -- two nested repetitions over
# overlapping character classes -- and on the long comment lines this tree is
# full of it backtracked exponentially: the census never finished a single
# file. A gate whose runtime depends on the shape of a comment is not a gate.
# So the C function rule splits on `(` in Python and matches only what is
# left of it, and the Swift rules match one alternation of keywords rather
# than an optional-modifier soup.
C_FUNC_HEAD = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_ \t\*]*$")
C_LAST_IDENT = re.compile(r"([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*$")
C_DEFINE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*#[ \t]*define[ \t]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)")
C_TAG = re.compile(
    r"^(?:typedef[ \t]+)?(struct|union|enum)[ \t]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)")
C_TYPEDEF_END = re.compile(r"^\}[ \t]*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)[ \t]*;")

SWIFT_KEYWORD = re.compile(
    r"\b(func|struct|class|enum|protocol|actor|extension|typealias)[ \t]+"
    r"([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)")
SWIFT_PROP = re.compile(r"\b(let|var)[ \t]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)")
SWIFT_CASE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*case[ \t]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)[ \t]*(?:$|[=(,:])")
# A declaration line may carry modifiers, but everything before the keyword
# must be one of these. That is what keeps `// a func named foo` and
# `if let x = y` out of the census without a lookbehind.
SWIFT_MODIFIERS = {
    "public", "internal", "private", "fileprivate", "open", "final",
    "static", "class", "override, ", "override", "mutating", "nonmutating",
    "nonisolated", "lazy", "weak", "unowned", "indirect", "convenience",
    "required", "dynamic", "optional", "@objc", "@discardableResult",
    "@inlinable", "@MainActor", "@Published", "@State", "@Binding",
    "@Environment", "@escaping", "@testable", "@available", "@usableFromInline",
}

PY_DEF = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*(def|class)[ \t]+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)")

MD_HEADING = re.compile(r"^(#{1,6})\s+(.*?)\s*#*\s*$")

# A conflicted blob from `git merge-tree` carries markers. Lines inside them
# are still real text on one side or the other, but the `=======` and the
# markers themselves are not, and neither is the count they would produce.
CONFLICT_MARKER = re.compile(r"^(<<<<<<<|=======|>>>>>>>|\|\|\|\|\|\|\|)")


def run(args, **kw):
    return subprocess.run(
        args, check=True, capture_output=True, text=True, **kw
    ).stdout


def repo_root():
    return run(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"]).strip()


def interesting(path):
    for p in SKIP_PREFIXES:
        if path.startswith(p) or ("/" + p) in path:
            return False
    return path.endswith(C_EXT + SWIFT_EXT + PY_EXT + MD_EXT)


def _swift_prefix_is_modifiers(prefix):
    """Is everything before a Swift keyword an access/behaviour modifier?

    This is the whole defence against counting prose. `func` inside a
    comment, `var` inside a string, `let` in the middle of an expression --
    all have something in front of them that is not a modifier.
    """
    for word in prefix.split():
        if word not in SWIFT_MODIFIERS:
            return False
    return True


def symbols_in(path, text):
    """(kind, name) pairs a file declares. Derived, never listed."""
    out = set()
    lines = text.split("\n")

    if path.endswith(C_EXT):
        for line in lines:
            if CONFLICT_MARKER.match(line):
                continue
            m = C_DEFINE.match(line)
            if m:
                out.add(("macro", m.group(1)))
                continue
            m = C_TAG.match(line)
            if m:
                out.add((m.group(1), m.group(2)))
                continue
            m = C_TYPEDEF_END.match(line)
            if m:
                out.add(("type", m.group(1)))
                continue
            if (line[:1].isalpha() or line[:1] == "_") and "(" in line:
                head = line.split("(", 1)[0]
                if C_FUNC_HEAD.match(head):
                    m = C_LAST_IDENT.search(head)
                    if m and m.group(1) not in C_NOT_A_NAME:
                        out.add(("func", m.group(1)))

    elif path.endswith(SWIFT_EXT):
        for line in lines:
            if CONFLICT_MARKER.match(line):
                continue
            m = SWIFT_KEYWORD.search(line)
            if m and _swift_prefix_is_modifiers(line[:m.start()]):
                out.add((m.group(1), m.group(2)))
                continue
            m = SWIFT_PROP.search(line)
            if m and _swift_prefix_is_modifiers(line[:m.start()]):
                out.add(("prop", m.group(2)))
                continue
            m = SWIFT_CASE.match(line)
            if m:
                out.add(("case", m.group(1)))

    elif path.endswith(PY_EXT):
        for line in lines:
            if CONFLICT_MARKER.match(line):
                continue
            m = PY_DEF.match(line)
            if m:
                out.add((m.group(1), m.group(2)))

    elif path.endswith(MD_EXT):
        fenced = False
        for line in lines:
            if line.startswith("```"):
                fenced = not fenced
                continue
            if fenced or CONFLICT_MARKER.match(line):
                continue
            m = MD_HEADING.match(line)
            if m:
                out.add(("heading", m.group(2)))

    return out


def census(treeish, root=None):
    """{path: {(kind, name), ...}} for one tree.

    One `git cat-file --batch` for the whole tree: reading 700 blobs with
    700 `git show` calls takes a minute, and a gate nobody waits for is a
    gate nobody runs.
    """
    listing = run(["git", "ls-tree", "-r", "-z", treeish], cwd=root)
    wanted = []
    for entry in listing.split("\0"):
        if not entry:
            continue
        meta, _, path = entry.partition("\t")
        parts = meta.split()
        if len(parts) < 3 or parts[1] != "blob":
            continue
        if interesting(path):
            wanted.append((parts[2], path))

    if not wanted:
        return {}

    proc = subprocess.Popen(
        ["git", "cat-file", "--batch"],
        stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, cwd=root,
    )
    stdout, _ = proc.communicate(
        b"".join((h + "\n").encode() for h, _ in wanted)
    )

    out = {}
    pos = 0
    for _, path in wanted:
        nl = stdout.index(b"\n", pos)
        header = stdout[pos:nl].decode()
        size = int(header.split()[2])
        body = stdout[nl + 1:nl + 1 + size]
        pos = nl + 1 + size + 1
        syms = symbols_in(path, body.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
        if syms:
            out[path] = syms
    return out


def flatten(c):
    return {(path, kind, name)
            for path, syms in c.items() for kind, name in syms}


def compare(before, after, root=None):
    """Symbols `before` had that `after` does not, minus whole-file moves.

    A path that is gone entirely is reported once as a file, not once per
    symbol: a rename that drops forty names is one decision, and forty
    lines of output about it would bury the one-line losses this exists to
    surface.
    """
    cb = census(before, root)
    ca = census(after, root)
    gone_files = sorted(set(cb) - set(ca))
    lost = sorted(
        (p, k, n) for (p, k, n) in flatten(cb) - flatten(ca) if p in ca
    )
    return cb, ca, lost, gone_files


def by_file(lost):
    out = {}
    for path, kind, name in lost:
        out.setdefault(path, []).append((kind, name))
    return out


def report_losses(title, lost, gone_files, stream=None):
    # Resolved at CALL time, not at def time. Binding sys.stdout in the
    # signature makes this immune to `redirect_stdout`, which is how the
    # gate's own tests first read its output as empty and passed a merge
    # they had just made lose two functions.
    p = (stream or sys.stdout).write
    p("\n%s\n" % title)
    if not lost and not gone_files:
        p("    nothing\n")
        return
    for path, items in sorted(by_file(lost).items()):
        p("    %s  (%d)\n" % (path, len(items)))
        for kind, name in sorted(items):
            p("        %-8s %s\n" % (kind, name))
    for path in gone_files:
        p("    %s  -- FILE GONE\n" % path)


def parents_of(commit, root=None):
    line = run(["git", "rev-list", "-1", "--parents", commit], cwd=root).split()
    return line[1:]


def cmd_census(args):
    root = repo_root()
    treeish = args[0] if args else "HEAD"
    c = census(treeish, root)
    total = sum(len(v) for v in c.values())
    for path in sorted(c):
        for kind, name in sorted(c[path]):
            print("%s\t%s\t%s" % (path, kind, name))
    print("# %d symbols in %d files" % (total, len(c)), file=sys.stderr)
    return 0


def cmd_compare(args):
    if len(args) != 2:
        print("usage: merge-census-gate compare <before> <after>",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    root = repo_root()
    _, _, lost, gone = compare(args[0], args[1], root)
    report_losses("symbols in %s and not in %s:" % (args[0], args[1]),
                  lost, gone)
    return 1 if (lost or gone) else 0


def cmd_check(args):
    """The merge-time verdict. `check` with no argument reads HEAD.

    Called from .githooks/post-merge, HEAD is the merge that just landed;
    called by hand on any merge commit it says the same thing about it.
    A non-merge commit is not this gate's business and passes silently.
    """
    root = repo_root()
    strict = "--strict" in args
    args = [a for a in args if not a.startswith("--")]
    commit = args[0] if args else "HEAD"

    parents = parents_of(commit, root)
    if len(parents) < 2:
        return 0

    sha = run(["git", "rev-parse", "--short", commit], cwd=root).strip()
    subject = run(["git", "log", "-1", "--format=%s", commit], cwd=root).strip()
    print("[merge-census] %s  %s" % (sha, subject))

    if len(parents) > 2:
        print("[merge-census] octopus merge: not covered, passing.")
        return 0

    base = run(["git", "merge-base", parents[0], parents[1]],
               cwd=root).strip()

    # First, and independent of any census: does this merge IMPORT a commit
    # that undoes its own predecessor without saying so? The census can only
    # see a revert that moved a NAME, and `fe4d8179` moved exactly one
    # (`Scene.swift`'s `cdef`) out of seven files' worth. Asking the other
    # gate directly about every commit the merge brings in does not depend
    # on that luck.
    merge_message = run(["git", "log", "-1", "--format=%B", commit],
                        cwd=root)
    imported = self_revert_suspects(parents, base, root, merge_message)

    result_census = census(commit, root)
    result = flatten(result_census)
    pc = [census(p, root) for p in parents]
    pf = [flatten(c) for c in pc]
    bf = flatten(census(base, root))

    # A symbol whose FILE the merge does not have is a file-level decision,
    # reported once below rather than once per name.
    live = set(result_census)

    def lost_from(i):
        return {s for s in pf[i] - result if s[0] in live}

    other = {0: 1, 1: 0}
    dropped_addition = []   # nobody removed it; the merge lost it
    honoured_removal = []   # the other side removed it after the base
    for i in (0, 1):
        for s in sorted(lost_from(i)):
            if s in bf and s not in pf[other[i]]:
                honoured_removal.append((other[i], s))
            else:
                dropped_addition.append((i, s))

    gone_files = sorted(set().union(*(set(c) for c in pc)) - live)

    if dropped_addition:
        for i in (0, 1):
            mine = [s for j, s in dropped_addition if j == i]
            if not mine:
                continue
            short = run(["git", "rev-parse", "--short", parents[i]],
                        cwd=root).strip()
            report_losses(
                "DROPPED -- parent %d (%s) has these, NOBODY removed them, "
                "and the merge does not:" % (i + 1, short), mine, [])

    # The bucket where a revert hides, so it is not printed as a bare list.
    # Each name is attributed to the commit that removed it, and that commit
    # is put to the self-revert gate: a removal inside a commit that undoes
    # its own predecessor without saying so is the shape that cost this
    # repository the CDEF attribution, and it is refusable where the bucket
    # as a whole is not.
    suspects = attribute_removals(honoured_removal, parents, base, root,
                                  merge_message)

    if gone_files:
        report_losses("files a parent had and the merge does not:",
                      [], gone_files)

    print()
    print("[merge-census] %d dropped, %d removed-by-a-side, %d files gone, "
          "%d imported self-reverts"
          % (len(dropped_addition), len(honoured_removal), len(gone_files),
             len(imported)))

    bad = False

    if imported:
        bad = True
        print()
        print("REFUSING. This merge imports %d commit(s) that undo their own"
              % len(imported))
        print("branch's predecessor in a message that never says so (above).")
        print("Merging one propagates the revert into the integration line,")
        print("where the next reader has no branch history to notice it in.")

    if dropped_addition:
        bad = True
        print()
        print("REFUSING on the DROPPED list. Neither side deleted these and")
        print("they are gone, so the resolution lost them. That is the")
        print("keep-both truncation this repository has paid for twice: the")
        print("shared trailing context sits OUTSIDE the conflict hunk, so a")
        print("resolution that keeps both sides of the hunk still loses it.")

    if suspects:
        bad = True
        print()
        print("REFUSING on a removal made by a commit that reverts its own")
        print("branch's predecessor without saying so (above). The merge is")
        print("propagating that revert into the integration line.")

    if honoured_removal and not suspects:
        print()
        print("The removed-by-a-side list is NOT refused and cannot be: a")
        print("deliberate deletion and a revert are the same bytes at merge")
        print("time. Each name above is attributed to the commit that removed")
        print("it, which is the part a reader can judge -- `fe4d8179` removed")
        print("seven files' worth in a commit whose subject was about")
        print("`hello.machine`.")
        if strict:
            print("--strict: refusing on it too.")
            bad = True

    return 1 if bad else 0


def load_self_revert(root):
    """`tools/self-revert-gate` as a module, not a subprocess.

    It has no `.py` on it, like every other tool here, so it is loaded by
    path. In-process matters: the sibling memoises its per-commit diffs,
    and one subprocess per commit throws that cache away between every
    one -- which turned a merge check into a two-minute wait, and a gate
    people wait two minutes for is a gate people turn off.
    """
    # Beside THIS file first. `root` is whatever repository is being
    # examined, and that is frequently not the one these tools live in --
    # a fixture repo in TMPDIR, or a clone being inspected from outside.
    # The sibling gate is part of this gate, not of the tree under test.
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
    for path in (os.path.join(here, "self-revert-gate"),
                 os.path.join(root, "tools", "self-revert-gate")):
        if os.path.exists(path):
            break
    else:
        return None
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(
        "now_self_revert_gate",
        importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("now_self_revert_gate", path))
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def self_revert_suspects(parents, base, root, merge_message=""):
    """Commits this merge IMPORTS that `tools/self-revert-gate` flags.

    `parents[0]..parents[n]` -- what the merge adds to the line it lands
    on -- and not `base..parents[0]`, which is history that already landed.
    Re-refusing that would make every subsequent merge in an arc red for a
    commit no one on this merge can now change, which is how a gate gets
    switched off rather than obeyed.
    """
    mod = load_self_revert(root)
    if mod is None:
        print("[merge-census] tools/self-revert-gate missing; the "
              "imported-commit half of this gate DID NOT RUN.")
        return []

    suspects = []
    for p in parents[1:]:
        rng = "%s..%s" % (parents[0], p)
        for c in run(["git", "rev-list", "--no-merges", rng],
                     cwd=root).split():
            found = mod.findings(c, root)
            if not found:
                continue
            # The same escape the sibling gate offers, at the merge's own
            # level: a merge that NAMES the revert it is carrying has said
            # what it is doing, and that is all this gate ever asks for.
            # Round 7's real merge of `claude/019-asset-packs` names
            # `fe4d8179` in its body and resolves all seven files by hand,
            # and a gate that still refused it would be flagging the branch
            # rather than the defect -- red on every merge of that lane
            # forever, which is a gate people route around.
            if mod.mentions(merge_message, c):
                continue
            suspects.append(c)
            print()
            print("IMPORTED SELF-REVERT:")
            buf = []
            mod.describe(c, found, root, quiet=False, emit=buf.append)
            for line in buf:
                print("    %s" % line)
    return suspects


def attribute_removals(honoured, parents, base, root, merge_message=""):
    """Which commit removed each name, and is that commit a self-revert?

    `git log -S` over the range that side owns answers the first question
    exactly, and it is the answer that makes this bucket readable: 80 bare
    names is a list nobody reads, and 80 names under six commit subjects is
    six judgements.
    """
    if not honoured:
        return []

    per_commit = {}
    for side, (path, kind, name) in honoured:
        # The range goes BEFORE `--`, and getting that wrong is not a
        # failure you see: `git log -S x -- <path> <range>` reads the range
        # as a second PATHSPEC, matches nothing, silently falls back to
        # HEAD's whole history, and answers with the commit that ADDED the
        # symbol instead of the one that removed it. It named `8bb266b5`
        # ("a button family is not a push button") as the remover of the
        # very key that commit introduced.
        rng = "%s..%s" % (base, parents[side])
        try:
            log = run(["git", "log", "-S", name, "--format=%H", rng,
                       "--", path], cwd=root)
        except subprocess.CalledProcessError:
            log = ""
        sha = log.split("\n")[0].strip() if log.strip() else "?"
        per_commit.setdefault(sha, []).append((path, kind, name))

    mod = load_self_revert(root)
    suspects = []
    print("\nremoved by a side, attributed to the commit that did it:")
    for sha, items in sorted(per_commit.items(),
                             key=lambda kv: -len(kv[1])):
        if sha == "?":
            subject = "(could not attribute)"
            short = "?"
        else:
            subject = run(["git", "log", "-1", "--format=%s", sha],
                          cwd=root).strip()
            short = run(["git", "rev-parse", "--short", sha],
                        cwd=root).strip()
        verdict = ""
        found = mod.findings(sha, root) if (sha != "?" and mod) else []
        # The merge's own message excuses it here exactly as it does in
        # self_revert_suspects. Two places that ask the same question have
        # to answer it the same way, or a merge passes one and is refused
        # by the other for the same commit.
        if found and mod.mentions(merge_message, sha):
            found = []
        if found:
            verdict = "   <-- SELF-REVERT"
            suspects.append(sha)
        print("    %s  %-58s (%d)%s"
              % (short, subject[:58], len(items), verdict))
        for path, kind, name in sorted(items):
            print("        %-8s %-28s %s" % (kind, name, path))
        if found:
            buf = []
            mod.describe(sha, found, root, quiet=True, emit=buf.append)
            for line in buf:
                print("        | %s" % line)
    return suspects


def cmd_pending(args):
    """The merge that has NOT been committed yet -- what a hook can refuse.

    `.githooks/pre-merge-commit` runs while the index holds the resolution
    and `MERGE_HEAD` holds the other parent, so the merge commit git is
    about to write can be built here exactly: `write-tree` for the content,
    both parents, and `MERGE_MSG` for the message. Then this is the same
    `check` as any other, on the same shape of object, rather than a
    second implementation that could disagree with it.
    """
    root = repo_root()
    merge_head = os.path.join(root, ".git", "MERGE_HEAD")
    if not os.path.exists(merge_head):
        gitdir = run(["git", "rev-parse", "--git-dir"], cwd=root).strip()
        merge_head = os.path.join(root, gitdir, "MERGE_HEAD")
    if not os.path.exists(merge_head):
        return 0                       # not a merge; nothing to say
    others = open(merge_head).read().split()

    msg_path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(merge_head), "MERGE_MSG")
    message = open(msg_path).read() if os.path.exists(msg_path) else ""

    tree = run(["git", "write-tree"], cwd=root).strip()
    head = run(["git", "rev-parse", "HEAD"], cwd=root).strip()
    cmd = ["git", "commit-tree", tree, "-p", head]
    for o in others:
        cmd += ["-p", o]
    cmd += ["-m", message or "pending merge"]
    pending = run(cmd, cwd=root).strip()
    return cmd_check([pending] + args)


def main(argv):
    if len(argv) < 2:
        print(__doc__)
        return 2
    cmds = {
        "census": cmd_census,
        "compare": cmd_compare,
        "check": cmd_check,
        "pending": cmd_pending,
    }
    fn = cmds.get(argv[1])
    if fn is None:
        print("unknown subcommand: %s" % argv[1], file=sys.stderr)
        print(__doc__)
        return 2
    return fn(argv[2:])


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
